urpureo's military victories in 200 BCE and his consular campaigns in 196 BCE directly led to greater Roman control over Cisalpine Gaul."}, {"sourceSlug": "triumph-roman-republic", "sourceName": "Triumph (Roman Republic)", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "lucius-furius-purpureo", "targetName": "Lucius Furius Purpureo", "context": "Lucius Furius Purpureo was awarded a prestigious Roman Triumph in 199 BCE for his decisive victory over the Gauls at Cremona."}], "places": [{"name": "Cremona, Roman Cisalpine Gaul", "role": "Site of major military victory"}, {"name": "Rome, Roman Republic", "role": "Political center and origin"}, {"name": "Macedonia, Hellenistic World", "role": "Province of command"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Lucius Furius Purpureo", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "lucius-furius-purpureo"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q451220", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Furius_Purpureo"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Lucius Furius Purpureo's military and political actions were critical in securing Roman dominance over Cisalpine Gaul, a region vital for Rome's expansion and security. His victories directly contributed to the consolidation of Roman power in northern Italy, influencing subsequent Roman colonization and infrastructure development.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T14:46:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29930003270", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Lucius Furius Purpureo was a Roman politician and general, becoming consul in the year 196 BC. Lucius Furius was from the gens Furia patrician family in Rome.", "newValue": "Lucius Furius Purpureo, a prominent Roman politician and general, played a crucial role in the Roman Republic's expansion during the late 3rd and early 2nd centuries BCE. Hailing from the ancient patrician gens Furia, his career culminated in the consulship of 196 BCE. Purpureo's military prowess was particularly evident in Cisalpine Gaul and during the Second Macedonian War, contributing significantly to Rome's consolidation of power in northern Italy and its growing influence in the Hellenistic East.

His military career began with distinction as a military tribune in 207 BCE under consul Gaius Claudius Nero. A pivotal moment came during his praetorship in 200 BCE, when he led Roman legions to a decisive victory over the Insubres and Cenomani Gauls at the Battle of Cremona. This engagement, fought near the Po River, saw him defeat the Carthaginian general Hamilcar, effectively quelling a major Gallic uprising and securing Roman control over a strategically vital region. For this triumph, Purpureo was awarded a spectacular triumph in Rome, an honor that underscored his military achievements and elevated his political standing.

As consul in 196 BCE, Purpureo continued to prosecute the war against the Gauls in Cisalpine Gaul, further extending Roman hegemony. His actions, alongside those of his consular colleague Marcus Claudius Marcellus, were instrumental in integrating the fertile lands of Cisalpine Gaul into the Roman sphere, paving the way for Roman colonization and the construction of vital infrastructure like the Via Aemilia. This consolidation not only provided Rome with agricultural resources and manpower but also secured its northern frontier against future incursions, fundamentally altering the demographic and political landscape of northern Italy."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-22T14:46:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29930003270", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "229 BCE, Ancient Rome"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "229 BCE, Ancient Rome"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Roman expansionist policies necessitated skilled military commanders and effective administrators.", "Persistent Gallic uprisings in Cisalpine Gaul demanded a robust Roman military response to secure the region.", "The demands of the Second Macedonian War required experienced Roman generals to manage concurrent military theaters."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-22T14:46:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29930003270", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "200 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Lucius Furius Purpureo's victory at Cremona significantly advanced Roman control and pacification of Cisalpine Gaul.", "His consulship contributed to the establishment of R